Using Ansible Tower / AWX
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure AWX (the open-source version of Ansible Tower) on a Linux system
Set up a project in AWX to manage Ansible playbooks
Create and configure an inventory to manage target hosts
Launch and monitor automation jobs through the AWX web interface
Understand the benefits of centralized automation management
Navigate the AWX dashboard and key features
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with Ansible concepts (playbooks, inventories, modules)
Knowledge of YAML syntax
Understanding of basic networking concepts
Experience with text editors like nano or vim
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides you with a ready-to-use Linux-based cloud machine. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment. The provided Linux machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required components during this lab.

Important Note: All tasks in this lab will be performed on a single Linux machine. We will simulate multiple hosts by using localhost and different user contexts.

Task 1: Install AWX (Ansible Tower Open Source)
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the System
First, let's update the system and install required dependencies.

# Update the system
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y curl wget git python3 python3-pip docker.io docker-compose

# Start and enable Docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

# Add current user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# Apply group changes (logout and login, or use newgrp)
newgrp docker
Subtask 1.2: Install Docker Compose
# Download and install Docker Compose
sudo curl -L "https://github.com/docker/compose/releases/latest/download/docker-compose-$(uname -s)-$(uname -m)" -o /usr/local/bin/docker-compose

# Make it executable
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/docker-compose

# Verify installation
docker-compose --version
Subtask 1.3: Clone AWX Repository
# Clone the AWX repository
cd /home/$USER
git clone https://github.com/ansible/awx.git
cd awx

# Switch to the latest stable release
git checkout $(git describe --tags $(git rev-list --tags --max-count=1))
Subtask 1.4: Install AWX using Docker Compose
# Navigate to the tools/docker-compose directory
cd tools/docker-compose

# Generate a secret key
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(50))" > .awx_secret_key

# Create inventory file for AWX installation
cat > inventory << 'EOF'
localhost ansible_connection=local ansible_python_interpreter="/usr/bin/env python3"

[all:vars]
dockerhub_base=ansible
awx_task_hostname=awx
awx_web_hostname=awxweb
postgres_data_dir="~/.awx/pgdocker"
host_port=80
host_port_ssl=443
docker_compose_dir="~/.awx/awxcompose"
pg_username=awx
pg_password=awxpass
pg_database=awx
pg_port=5432
admin_user=admin
admin_password=password
create_preload_data=True
secret_key_file=.awx_secret_key
EOF
Subtask 1.5: Deploy AWX
# Install Ansible if not already installed
pip3 install ansible

# Run the AWX installation playbook
ansible-playbook -i inventory install.yml

# Wait for containers to start (this may take several minutes)
echo "Waiting for AWX to start..."
sleep 60

# Check if containers are running
docker ps
Subtask 1.6: Verify AWX Installation
# Check AWX container logs
docker logs awx_task

# Wait for AWX to be fully ready
echo "Waiting for AWX web interface to be ready..."
until curl -s http://localhost > /dev/null; do
    echo "Still waiting..."
    sleep 10
done

echo "AWX is ready! Access it at http://localhost"
echo "Default credentials: admin / password"
Task 2: Set Up a Project and Create an Inventory
Subtask 2.1: Access AWX Web Interface
Open a web browser and navigate to http://localhost
Log in with the default credentials:
Username: admin
Password: password
Subtask 2.2: Create a Sample Playbook Repository
First, let's create a local Git repository with sample playbooks:

# Create a directory for our playbooks
mkdir -p /home/$USER/awx-playbooks
cd /home/$USER/awx-playbooks

# Initialize Git repository
git init

# Create a simple playbook
cat > site.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: System Information Gathering
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  tasks:
    - name: Display system information
      debug:
        msg: |
          Hostname: {{ ansible_hostname }}
          OS: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}
          Architecture: {{ ansible_architecture }}
          Memory: {{ ansible_memtotal_mb }} MB

    - name: Create a test file
      file:
        path: /tmp/awx-test-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}
        state: touch
        mode: '0644'
      register: file_created

    - name: Show created file
      debug:
        msg: "Created file: {{ file_created.dest }}"

    - name: Get current date
      command: date
      register: current_date

    - name: Display current date
      debug:
        msg: "Current date and time: {{ current_date.stdout }}"
EOF

# Create an inventory file
cat > inventory.ini << 'EOF'
[local]
localhost ansible_connection=local

[local:vars]
ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3
EOF

# Create a requirements file
cat > requirements.yml << 'EOF'
---
# No external requirements for this simple example
EOF

# Add files to git
git add .
git config user.email "student@example.com"
git config user.name "AWX Student"
git commit -m "Initial commit with sample playbook"
Subtask 2.3: Create a Project in AWX
In the AWX web interface, click on Projects in the left sidebar
Click the + button to add a new project
Fill in the project details:
Name: Sample Playbooks
Description: Sample playbooks for AWX demonstration
Organization: Default
SCM Type: Git
SCM URL: /home/$USER/awx-playbooks (local path)
SCM Branch/Tag/Commit: master
Check Update Revision on Launch
Click Save
Note: Since we're using a local repository, AWX will access it directly from the filesystem.

Subtask 2.4: Create an Inventory
Click on Inventories in the left sidebar
Click the + button and select Inventory
Fill in the inventory details:
Name: Local Inventory
Description: Inventory for local host management
Organization: Default
Click Save
Subtask 2.5: Add Hosts to Inventory
After creating the inventory, click on it to open the details
Click on the Hosts tab
Click the + button to add a host
Fill in the host details:
Host Name: localhost
Description: Local machine
In the Variables section, add:
ansible_connection: local
ansible_python_interpreter: /usr/bin/python3
Click Save
Subtask 2.6: Create a Group (Optional)
In the inventory, click on the Groups tab
Click the + button to add a group
Fill in the group details:
Name: local_servers
Description: Local server group
Click Save
Click on the group, then go to Hosts tab
Click + and select Existing Host
Select localhost and click Save
Task 3: Launch Jobs from the Tower UI
Subtask 3.1: Create a Job Template
Click on Templates in the left sidebar
Click the + button and select Job Template
Fill in the template details:
Name: System Info Job
Description: Gather system information from local host
Job Type: Run
Inventory: Local Inventory
Project: Sample Playbooks
Playbook: site.yml
Credentials: Leave empty (using local connection)
Under Options, check:
Enable Privilege Escalation (if needed)
Enable Fact Storage
Click Save
Subtask 3.2: Launch Your First Job
From the Templates page, find your System Info Job template
Click the Launch button (rocket icon)
The job will start immediately and you'll be redirected to the job details page
Watch the job execution in real-time:
Status: Shows current job status
Output: Shows live output from the playbook execution
Details: Shows job metadata
Subtask 3.3: Monitor Job Execution
Observe the job output as it runs
Note the different sections:
PLAY RECAP: Summary of task results
Task details: Individual task execution
Timing information: How long each task took
Subtask 3.4: View Job History
Click on Jobs in the left sidebar
You'll see a list of all executed jobs
Click on your completed job to view details
Explore different tabs:
Details: Job metadata and settings
Output: Complete job output
Facts: Gathered facts (if fact caching is enabled)
Subtask 3.5: Create and Launch a Second Job Template
Let's create another playbook and job template:

# Create another playbook
cd /home/$USER/awx-playbooks

cat > disk-usage.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Disk Usage Report
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: no
  tasks:
    - name: Check disk usage
      shell: df -h
      register: disk_usage

    - name: Display disk usage
      debug:
        msg: "{{ disk_usage.stdout_lines }}"

    - name: Check memory usage
      shell: free -h
      register: memory_usage

    - name: Display memory usage
      debug:
        msg: "{{ memory_usage.stdout_lines }}"

    - name: List running processes
      shell: ps aux | head -10
      register: processes

    - name: Display top processes
      debug:
        msg: "{{ processes.stdout_lines }}"
EOF

# Commit the new playbook
git add disk-usage.yml
git commit -m "Add disk usage playbook"
Now create a new job template in AWX:

Go to Templates and click + → Job Template
Fill in the details:
Name: Disk Usage Report
Description: Generate system resource usage report
Job Type: Run
Inventory: Local Inventory
Project: Sample Playbooks
Playbook: disk-usage.yml
Click Save
Launch the job and observe the output
Subtask 3.6: Schedule a Job (Optional)
Go to your System Info Job template
Click Schedules tab
Click + to add a schedule
Fill in schedule details:
Name: Daily System Check
Start Date/Time: Set to current time + 2 minutes
Repeat Frequency: Run Every → Minute
Interval: 5 (runs every 5 minutes)
Click Save
Note: For demonstration purposes, we set a short interval. In production, you'd typically use longer intervals.

Subtask 3.7: Explore AWX Dashboard
Click on Dashboard in the left sidebar
Explore the different widgets:
